{{also|Töten|toten|Toten}}
==German==

===Alternative forms===
* {{alter|de|tödten||obsolete|predominant until 1900}}, {{alter|de|töden||until 18th c.}}

===Etymology===
{{dercat|de|gem-pro|inh=1}}
From {{der|de|gmh|tœten}}, {{m|gmh|tœden}}, from {{der|de|goh||*tōten}}, {{m|goh|tōden}}, from {{inh|de|gmw-pro|*daudijan|*daudijan ~ *dauþijan}}. {{surface analysis|de|tot|t1=dead|-en}}.

Cognate with {{cog|nl|doden}}, {{cog|non|deyða}}, {{cog|got|𐌳𐌰𐌿𐌸𐌾𐌰𐌽}}. Compare also {{cog|en|deaden}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|de|/ˈtøːtən/|[ˈtøː.tn̩]}}
* {{audio|de|De-töten.ogg}}
* {{hyph|de|tö|ten}}

===Verb===
{{de-verb}}

# {{lb|de|transitive}} to [[kill]]
#: {{ux|de|Zehn Menschen wurden bei dem Anschlag '''getötet'''.|t=Ten people were '''killed''' in the attack.}}

====Usage notes====
* In formal style, ''töten'' is an adequate translation of English “to kill” in its literal meaning. Colloquially, the verb {{m|de|umbringen}} is preferred and ''töten'' is rare. In particular, only ''umbringen'' is generally used in figurative senses. For example: {{m|de||Ich bring meinen Freund um, wenn er meine Blumen nicht gegossen hat!|t=I'll kill my boyfriend if he hasn't watered my flowers!}}.

